---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> A2: Briefly describe what happens in a call to timer_sleep(),
>> including the effects of the timer interrupt handler.

>> In timer_sleep(), the wake-up time of the thread is set, and then thread_sleep is called to put the thread to sleep.
>> And in thread_sleep(), the thread is inserted into the sleep list, min_ticks is updated, and then the thread is set to the blocked state.
>> In timer_interrupt(), thread_wakeup() is called.
thread_wakeup() unblocks the threads in the sleep_list whose wake-up time has arrived.

>> A3: What steps are taken to minimize the amount of time spent in
>> the timer interrupt handler?

>> In thread_sleep and thread_wakeup, min_ticks is updated to keep track of the next thread’s wake-up time.
>> When thread_wakeup is called, it compares the current tick with min_ticks. 
>> If it is not yet time to wake up any thread, the function terminates.
>> This way, unnecessary traversal of the sleep_list on every timer_interrupt call can be avoided.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: How are race conditions avoided when multiple threads call
>> timer_sleep() simultaneously?

>> In timer_sleep(), interrupts are disabled to prevent context switches while the function executes. 
>> This ensures that shared resources such as sleep_list and min_ticks are not accessed concurrently, thereby avoiding race conditions.

>> A5: How are race conditions avoided when a timer interrupt occurs
>> during a call to timer_sleep()?

>>Similarly, since interrupts are disabled, the timer interrupt is delayed, and timer_sleep is guaranteed atomicity.

